# tests/recursion/zerodef.rxt -- module `recursion` ([DD-14]): SS4.4c: the slot layout must count every EMITTED callee region, including one parked under `X{0}` -- plain/recursive/atomic/rung-bearing, each against its `{1}` non-{0} twin
#
# GENERATED BY tests/recursion/gen_corpus.py, and that is a property rather
# than a convenience: every expectation below -- including every `g` line --
# was produced by driving the cell through libpcre2 10.46 (via the committed
# ctypes binding at docs/design/subroutines_measurements/probes/sr_oracle.py,
# which borrows la_oracle.py -> br_oracle.py -> pcre2_ctypes.py) BEFORE it was
# written. The generator never asks pcrec anything.
#
# THE SOLE ORACLE IS LIBPCRE2 -- THERE IS NO PYTHON ARM, AND THAT IS AN
# ABSENCE RATHER THAN A DIVERGENCE (design SS10.1). python3 `re` has no
# subroutine-call construct at all: every one of the nine call spellings plus
# both zero spellings raises `re.error` at compile time. `\\1` and `(?P=n)`
# DO compile in python, but they are `backrefs`' reference construct, not this
# module's call construct (design SS2.1's one-cell discriminator is why
# `spellings.rxt` opens with it) -- so marking this corpus python-verifiable
# on the strength of those two spellings would be checking the wrong module.
#
# `(?(DEFINE)...)` IS THIS MODULE'S SINCE [DD-14] WAVE F (D71 item 4,
# overruling design SS2.5's "RULED: no DEFINE"). It is a TAILED row on the
# `(?(` doorway -- the rest of `(?(` is still `conditionals`' -- and it lowers
# to the SAME NODE the `{0}`-callee idiom produces, an A_REP with
# rmin == rmax == 0 over the body. That is why define.rxt and zerodef.rxt
# carry the two spellings SIDE BY SIDE on the same cells: they are two
# spellings of one construct, and the corpus asserts they agree rather than
# assuming it. Where a body must be callee-only, `{0}` remains the spelling
# used in the OTHER files, because those cells were oracle-verified in that
# form and re-spelling them would move a measurement without re-measuring it.
#
# NOTHING IN src/ IMPLEMENTS A SUBROUTINE CALL YET. Every ordinary `m`/`n`
# block in this corpus is therefore EXPECTED to report a pattern-compile
# failure under today's harness (`requires module 'recursion'`) -- that is
# the correct, documented state before [DD-14] wave B+C lands (docs/
# testing.md's "expected-unsupported" policy), not a corpus defect.
#
# Design: docs/design/subroutines_design.md. Decisions: docs/dev/decisions.md
# D71 (the give-up-code axis, D71.1) and D72 (PCREC_ERR_INTERNAL).

# ====================================================================
# PLAIN: a callee with only CAPTURE slots -- the CONTROL. `{0}` does not prune anything a plain callee needs, so this row is not load-bearing on its own (design SS4.4c/SS9.3 S-SR19).
# ====================================================================

# the classic pre-DEFINE idiom, parked under `{0}`.
pattern ^(?:(?<g>a|ab)){0}(?&g)c$
features recursion,named-groups
m "abc" 0 3
g 1 -1 -1

# [DD-14 wave F] THE DEFINE SPELLING OF THE CELL ABOVE, and the pair is the assertion: D71 item 4 lowers `(?(DEFINE)X)` to the node `(?:X){0}` produces, so the two cells must give the SAME answer and the same group. If they ever diverge, the lowering has grown a second mechanism.
pattern ^(?(DEFINE)(?<g>a|ab))(?&g)c$
features recursion,named-groups
m "abc" 0 3
g 1 -1 -1

# the NON-{0} CONTROL: the SAME callee, emitted lexically once (`{1}`) as well as called -- "abc" now fails because the LEXICAL copy must also consume.
pattern ^(?:(?<g>a|ab)){1}(?&g)c$
features recursion,named-groups
m "aabc" 0 4
g 1 0 1
n "abc"

# ====================================================================
# RECURSIVE: a callee whose own body calls itself, parked under `{0}` (design SS4.4c, axis Z0).
# ====================================================================

pattern ^(x)?(?:(?<h>a(?2)?b)){0}(?2)$
features recursion,named-groups
m "aabb" 0 4
g 1 -1 -1
g 2 -1 -1

# the DEFINE spelling: a RECURSIVE callee defined inside a DEFINE, which is the library idiom users actually write.
pattern ^(x)?(?(DEFINE)(?<h>a(?2)?b))(?2)$
features recursion,named-groups
m "aabb" 0 4
g 1 -1 -1
g 2 -1 -1

# the NON-{0} CONTROL: the lexical copy runs once (matching the first "aabb"), then the call re-runs it.
pattern ^(x)?(?:(?<h>a(?2)?b)){1}(?2)$
features recursion,named-groups
m "aabbaabb" 0 8
g 1 -1 -1
g 2 0 4

# ====================================================================
# ATOMIC -- LOAD-BEARING (design SS4.4c/SS9.3 S-SR19): an atomic callee allocates a CUT_MARK slot family that a lexical-only count would miss entirely once the callee sits under `{0}`.
# ====================================================================

pattern ^(?:((?>a|ab))){0}(?1)z$
features recursion,atomic-groups
m "az" 0 2
g 1 -1 -1
n "abz"

# the DEFINE spelling of the LOAD-BEARING atomic cell -- the CUT_MARK family a lexical-only slot count would miss has to be counted through this doorway too.
pattern ^(?(DEFINE)((?>a|ab)))(?1)z$
features recursion,atomic-groups
m "az" 0 2
g 1 -1 -1
n "abz"

# the NON-{0} CONTROL.
pattern ^(?:((?>a|ab))){1}(?1)z$
features recursion,atomic-groups
m "aaz" 0 3
g 1 0 1
n "aabz"

# ====================================================================
# RUNG-BEARING -- LOAD-BEARING (design SS4.4c/SS9.3 S-SR19): a callee whose OWN quantifier needs rung state (an empty-iteration guard family here), parked under `{0}`.
# ====================================================================

pattern ^(?:(a?)){0}(?1)*b$
features recursion
m "b" 0 1
g 1 -1 -1

# the DEFINE spelling of the LOAD-BEARING rung-bearing cell.
pattern ^(?(DEFINE)(a?))(?1)*b$
features recursion
m "b" 0 1
g 1 -1 -1

# the NON-{0} CONTROL.
pattern ^(?:(a?)){1}(?1)*b$
features recursion
m "ab" 0 2
g 1 0 1
m "aaab" 0 4
g 1 0 1
